.TH TAPEWORM 1 "June 2026" "tapeworm 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tapeworm \- TUI log file analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tapeworm
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tapeworm
is a terminal UI log file analyzer inspired by htop.
It watches log files in real time, parses them with regex profiles,
aggregates counts by level and field, detects anomalies, and exports
results to JSON or CSV.
.PP
The interface is split into a file list sidebar and a main view that
can show live tail output, aggregation summaries, or detected anomalies.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR FILE ...
One or more log files to watch. Each file must exist and be a regular
file. Multiple files are tracked in the sidebar and can be switched
between at runtime.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tick-rate " " \fIMILLISECONDS\fR
UI refresh interval in milliseconds. Lower values make the interface
more responsive at the cost of higher CPU usage. Default: 250.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-initial-lines " " \fIN\fR
Number of most recent lines to read from each file on startup.
Default: 50.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " " \fINAME\fR
Log parsing profile to use. Built\-in profiles are
.BR syslog ,
.BR nginx ,
.BR apache ,
and
.BR json .
A custom profile can be loaded by passing a path to a JSON profile
file. Default: syslog.
.TP
.B \-\-no-parse
Disable log parsing and show raw lines only.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-export " " \fIFORMAT\fR
On exit, export collected data to a file. Supported formats are
.B json
and
.BR csv .
When this option is used, data is written automatically when the
application exits.
.TP
.B \-\-export-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Output path for exported data. If omitted, the default is
.IR tapeworm-export.json
or
.IR tapeworm-export.csv
depending on the export format.
.TP
.B \-\-theme " " \fINAME\fR
Color theme for the UI. Available themes:
.BR default ,
.BR dark ,
.BR light ,
.BR solarized ,
.BR monokai .
Default: default.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROLS
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl+C
Quit
.BR tapeworm .
.TP
.B Tab
Switch focus between the file list and the main view.
.TP
.BR j ", " \(Do
Next file when the file list is focused, or scroll up in the main view.
.TP
.BR k ", " \(Fo
Previous file when the file list is focused, or scroll down in the main view.
.TP
.B G
Scroll to the bottom of the tail view.
.TP
.B g
Scroll to the top of the tail view.
.TP
.B a
Toggle the main view mode: Tail \(-> Aggregation \(-> Anomaly.
.TP
.B t
Cycle the aggregation time window: 1m \(-> 5m \(-> 15m \(-> 1h.
.TP
.B T
Cycle the active color theme.
.TP
.B p
Toggle parsed and raw line display.
.TP
.BR h ", " ?
Toggle the keyboard shortcuts help popup.
.TP
.B Esc
Close the help popup.
.SH PROFILES
Profiles define how log lines are parsed into structured fields and
log levels. Built\-in profiles cover common formats, and custom profiles
can be written as JSON files.
.PP
A profile JSON file contains:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR name :
profile name
.IP \(bu 2
.BR regex :
PCRE\-style regular expression with named capture groups
.IP \(bu 2
.BR level_field :
optional capture group that contains the log level
.IP \(bu 2
.BR timestamp_field :
optional capture group that contains the timestamp
.IP \(bu 2
.BR fields :
list of additional field names to extract from capture groups
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Watch a single log file with the default syslog profile:
.RS
.nf
$ tapeworm /var/log/syslog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch multiple files using the nginx profile:
.RS
.nf
$ tapeworm --profile nginx /var/log/nginx/access.log /var/log/nginx/error.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a faster tick rate and read more initial lines:
.RS
.nf
$ tapeworm --tick-rate 100 --initial-lines 200 /var/log/syslog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export to JSON on exit:
.RS
.nf
$ tapeworm --export json --export-output /tmp/syslog.json /var/log/syslog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a custom profile and the monokai theme:
.RS
.nf
$ tapeworm --theme monokai --profile /path/to/my-profile.json /var/log/app.log
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I profiles/*.json
Built\-in log parsing profiles shipped with the application.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
No environment variables are required. The application runs in the
user\(aqs terminal using crossterm and should work in any common
terminal emulator.
